I am using computers in public libraries because when I use a computer of my own, the hackers can control even the mouse. 

Since the Chrome version 151, in this computer 151.0.7922.109 (Official Build) (64-bit) (cohort: Stable) , but I have observed the same behavior in another computer that has a version like 151.0.7922.110, for any given day (keep in mind that apparently the computer are wiped. So each new day, we can presume a clean slate), after the first print or download, the Save button of the file picker that opens if I want to save a printout PDF or download a file, is unresponsive on the first try. I am equired to click again for the button to work. After this second use of the Save button, the anomalous route (i.e., I click the second time), then the browser appears to work as before. 

From my experience with persistent hacking, I have learned that coincidences are unlikely, and that behavior that is out of line of proper functioning is a symptom of a hacked device. So I think that this unresponsive Save button on the second use for the day, and which requires a second Enter key press, may be a way for the hackers to leak memory and bypass whatever security Chrome offers. 

For me, this means no printing of hashed records for changed files through the browser, and no screen recordings through Chrome. Perhaps I end up using Firefox. I am unsure. 

I have reported this to staff I see most associated to IT related matters here in the library. 

This screenrecording illustrates what I am trying to describe: https://cybertrapped.substack.com/p/chrome-15107922110-and-15107922109

At the videos timestamp 00:00:51.110, after I pressed the Enter key for the first time, instead of Saving the file, the cursor backtracks to the FIlePickers filename field. This is why the background of the filename field is AGAIN highlighted with a blue background.

